WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.370 --> 00:00:04.640
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.640 --> 00:00:08.670
When we are talking about deep learning, we
are talking about neural networks that have

00:00:08.670 --> 00:00:14.059
tens, sometimes hundreds of layers, and hundreds
of neurons within these layers.

00:00:14.059 --> 00:00:19.530
This is an enormous number of parameters to
train, and clearly, there should be some redundancy,

00:00:19.530 --> 00:00:21.750
some duplication in the information within.

00:00:21.750 --> 00:00:26.470
This paper is trying to throw out many of
these neurons of the network without affecting

00:00:26.470 --> 00:00:28.030
its accuracy too much.

00:00:28.030 --> 00:00:33.559
This process we shall call pruning and it
helps creating neural networks that are faster

00:00:33.559 --> 00:00:34.600
and smaller.

00:00:34.600 --> 00:00:40.190
The accuracy term I used typically means a
score on a classification task, in other words,

00:00:40.190 --> 00:00:45.579
how good this learning algorithm is in telling
what an image or video depicts.

00:00:45.579 --> 00:00:50.170
This particular technique is specialized for
pruning Convolutional Neural Networks, where

00:00:50.170 --> 00:00:56.000
the neurons are endowed with a small receptive
field and are better suited for images.

00:00:56.000 --> 00:00:59.469
These neurons are also commonly referred to
as filters.

00:00:59.469 --> 00:01:04.620
So here, we have to provide a good mathematical
definition of a proper pruning.

00:01:04.620 --> 00:01:10.100
The authors proposed a definition where we
can specify a maximum accuracy drop that we

00:01:10.100 --> 00:01:14.770
deem to be acceptable, which will be denoted
with the letter "b" in a moment.

00:01:14.770 --> 00:01:20.050
And the goal is to prune as many filters as
we can, without going over the specified accuracy

00:01:20.050 --> 00:01:21.200
loss budget.

00:01:21.200 --> 00:01:26.540
The pruning process is controlled by an accuracy
and efficiency term, and the goal is to have

00:01:26.540 --> 00:01:28.880
some sort of balance between the two.

00:01:28.880 --> 00:01:34.110
To get a more visual understanding of what
is happening, here, the filters you see outlined

00:01:34.110 --> 00:01:39.180
with the red border are kept by the algorithm,
and the rest are discarded.

00:01:39.180 --> 00:01:44.260
As you can see, the algorithm is not as trivial
as many previous approaches that just prune

00:01:44.260 --> 00:01:46.700
away filters with weaker responses.

00:01:46.700 --> 00:01:49.560
Here you see the table with the b numbers.

00:01:49.560 --> 00:01:55.010
Initial tests reveal that around a quarter
of the filters can be pruned with an accuracy

00:01:55.010 --> 00:02:02.710
loss of 0.3%, and with a higher b, we can
prune more than 75% of the filters with a

00:02:02.710 --> 00:02:04.970
loss of around 3%.

00:02:04.970 --> 00:02:06.990
This is incredible.

00:02:06.990 --> 00:02:12.050
Image segmentation tasks are about finding
the regions that different objects inhabit.

00:02:12.050 --> 00:02:16.799
Interestingly, when trying the pruning for
this task, it not only introduces a minimal

00:02:16.799 --> 00:02:22.370
loss of accuracy, in some cases, the pruned
version of the neural network performs even

00:02:22.370 --> 00:02:23.370
better.

00:02:23.370 --> 00:02:24.859
How cool is that!

00:02:24.859 --> 00:02:28.930
And of course, the best part is that we can
choose a tradeoff that is appropriate for

00:02:28.930 --> 00:02:30.130
our application.

00:02:30.130 --> 00:02:34.700
For instance, if we are we looking for a light
cleanup, we can use the first option at a

00:02:34.700 --> 00:02:40.549
minimal penalty, or, if we wish to have a
tiny tiny neural network that can run on a

00:02:40.549 --> 00:02:46.170
mobile device, we can look for the more heavy-handed
approach by sacrificing just a tiny bit more

00:02:46.170 --> 00:02:47.170
accuracy.

00:02:47.170 --> 00:02:48.959
And, we have everything in between.

00:02:48.959 --> 00:02:53.700
There is plenty more validation for the method
in the paper, make sure to have a look!

00:02:53.700 --> 00:02:58.739
It is really great to see that new research
works make neural networks not only more powerful

00:02:58.739 --> 00:03:03.760
over time, but there are efforts in making
them smaller and more efficient at the same

00:03:03.760 --> 00:03:04.840
time.

00:03:04.840 --> 00:03:05.840
Great news indeed.

00:03:05.840 --> 00:03:09.920
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

